MAJOR_VERSION=5
STANDARD_VERSION=2
RELEASE_VERSION=0
DESCRIPTION=Add `netlab`, a self-contained network-security teaching lab exposed as a userland shell command (userland/command/cmd_netlab.c, with a freestanding analog in kernel/freestanding/x86_64/commands.c for the browser kernel): simulated "pods" of fabricated hosts (SSID/IP/MAC/role), ASCII topology, a simulated traceroute and frame-monitor, an ARP-spoof/MiTM walkthrough, a reverse-shell explainer, a classic libpcap writer that serializes the simulated frame script, and a defensive pcap analyzer that parses a real capture and flags duplicate IP->MAC bindings as an ARP-spoofing signature. Pod membership is keyed on the real logged-in account (not a free-typed name) with host-collision avoidance -- a member hosting the real `server` session never also holds the pod host role, re-resolved at pod create/join/leave and at accept time -- and a listen/accept reverse-shell binding (listener=attacker, acceptor=victim; self-accept rejected) redirects the attacker's non-management verbs into a disposable, isolated in-memory sandbox labeled for the victim that runs real commands (whoami/hostname/id/uname/pwd/ifconfig/netstat/ls/write/cat/rm) against its own throwaway state -- never the victim's real session, files, or terminal -- with either side able to end the binding and the real network paused while it is open. Server lifecycle is coupled to pods, treating a pod host and the server host as two distinct relay points that one session can never hold at once: `server host` re-elects the pod host off the new server-host session on the spot (cmd_netlab_on_server_host / freestanding nl_pick_host from the server-host path), `server kill` by the server host dissolves the pods that lived under it (cmd_netlab_on_server_kill / freestanding nl_pod_dissolve), and `server leave` by a host that is also a pod host re-elects the pod host by pod join order independently of the server's own host succession, so the two new hosts may differ (cmd_netlab_on_server_leave / freestanding nl_pick_host from the server-leave path). A single account may still hold both roles via two sessions (two relay points, shown as `name {N}`); the exclusion is keyed on the session, not the account. The module is deliberately incapable of live-attack behavior: it is libc-only, opens no sockets, reads no real interface, intercepts no one else's traffic, injects into no one else's real terminal or session, and forges no real capture; it teaches the concepts and the detection/prevention half of the source material instead. Wiring: FL_SCMD_NETLAB in fl_shell_cmd.h, registry/dispatch/batch-dispatch entries, cmd_decl.h declarations (including cmd_netlab_is_isolated and cmd_server_is_running), a help block in userland/shell/common.c, a libc-only unit test (tests/test_netlab_sim.c) covering the verbs, the real-account pod/listen/accept/sandbox flow, and the simulated->pcap->analyze round trip, and a `make test_netlab_sim` target wired into test_all via scripts/run_all_tests.sh. Browser-lab GUI (tools/browser-lab/index.html + lab.js) drives the new pod/listen/accept controls with no free-typed identity. The whole lab is gated behind a `SECFEAT` build flag (default off) so a plain `make`, `main`, and the Flinstone page ship a shell with no security lab: the flag defines FL_SECFEAT and guards the registration/dispatch/help call sites and the freestanding netlab region, the Makefile drops cmd_netlab.c from the default link, and the browser kernel builds two images (dist/flintstone.img lab-free, dist/flintstone-secfeat.img via `make browser-kernel-secfeat` for the MitM+RS page). Docs in docs/NETLAB.md.

RELEASE_DATE=2026-10-12
